    "description": "Public-space design shapes the routes and shared places through which people encounter a city. Isfahan's Naqsh-e Jahan Square and Tehran's Tabiat Bridge illustrate different ways of combining movement, gathering, and surrounding urban functions.",
    "tags": ["Public space", "Urban design", "Pedestrians", "Isfahan", "Tehran", "Tabiat Bridge"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:20:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:20:30Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Public Space Design in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Read Iranian public-space design through Isfahan's historic square and Tehran's Tabiat Bridge, considering movement, gathering, and evidence limits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-public-space",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Movement, gathering, and the urban setting",
        "content": "Public-space design can be understood through the relationship between a shared place and the activities around it. Isfahan's Naqsh-e Jahan Square brings monumental buildings and commercial arcades into a single composition. Tehran's Tabiat Bridge connects parks across a highway while providing places to pause. Together, they offer contrasting examples of how circulation and gathering can occupy the same urban setting. [s1][s2]\n\nThese examples should be read at their own scales. A historic civic square and a contemporary pedestrian bridge answer different briefs and belong to different periods. Their value for comparison lies in the questions they raise: how people arrive, what holds their attention, and how the surrounding uses give a shared space a purpose. This is an analytical comparison of documented designs, rather than a claim that either represents every Iranian public space. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "isfahan-square",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Naqsh-e Jahan: an urban ensemble",
        "content": "Naqsh-e Jahan, also listed by UNESCO as Meidan Emam, was created under Shah Abbas I in the early seventeenth century. Its perimeter links arcades with the Ali Qapu palace entrance, the Qeyssariyeh bazaar gateway, and the Royal and Sheikh Lotfallah mosques. The square therefore connects commercial, governmental, and religious settings within a coherent spatial arrangement. [s1]\n\nIts history also complicates any assumption that a grand open space was simply a place of leisure. UNESCO records promenades, celebrations, polo, troop assemblies, and public executions among its historical uses. Public space accommodated sociability and displays of authority. For a contemporary reader, that combination helps explain why architectural openness alone cannot establish how freely a space was used or who controlled its activities. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tabiat-bridge",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Tabiat Bridge: a crossing that invites a stay",
        "content": "Completed in 2014, Tabiat Bridge was designed by Diba Tensile Architecture, with Leila Araghian and Alireza Behzadi named in the Aga Khan Award project record. It links parks on opposite sides of Modarres Highway in Tehran. Its documented design goes beyond providing a direct crossing: a curved route, changing widths, multiple approaches, and seating make room for different ways of moving and pausing. [s2]\n\nThe award documentation describes planting, cafes, and covered space as parts of this inhabitable infrastructure. It also explains the effort to limit the structure's footprint among existing trees. The resulting design illustrates how transport infrastructure can incorporate a destination within the connection itself. However, the award account is an architectural appraisal; its positive descriptions do not substitute for an independent survey of accessibility or users' experiences. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evaluation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From design intention to everyday use",
        "content": "These cases support a practical distinction between spatial features and demonstrated social outcomes. Plans and project descriptions can establish where routes lead, which activities a design accommodates, and how buildings frame a place. Establishing who uses it comfortably requires additional evidence. Neither source supplies a comparable annual record of use, inclusion, or satisfaction. [s1][s2]\n\nA useful evaluation would therefore ask how the intended design works in practice: whether approaches are convenient, places to rest remain usable, and different visitors experience the setting differently. These are proposed evaluation questions, not measured findings about the two sites. The documented examples nevertheless show why public-space design deserves attention beyond visual appearance: it organizes the relationship between passing through a city and spending time within it. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "tabiat-spatial-relationships",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Connection and gathering at Tabiat Bridge",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Park approaches\"] -->|\"Lead pedestrians onto\"| B[\"Curved bridge route\"]\n  B -->|\"Provides a connection to\"| C[\"Park on the opposite side\"]\n  B -->|\"Includes\"| D[\"Seating and planted areas\"]\n  B -->|\"Includes\"| E[\"Covered space\"]\n  D -->|\"Designed to encourage\"| F[\"Pausing and gathering\"]\n  E -->|\"Provides shelter for\"| F\n```",
        "summary": "The diagram summarizes documented spatial features and design intentions. Its arrows describe access and provision, rather than measured effects on visitors.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
